WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.049
Film Courage: How do you improve or make a story
better?  Marty Lang, Screenwriter/Director and Assistant Professor of Film at the University of Central Arkansas/Director: Well there's a lot of ways that

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:10.830
you can make a story better the the most
effective way I've found is to make it

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:16.410
shorter the thing I've learned one of
the big things I learned at CSUN [California State University Northridge] was

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:23.010
that there can't be any superfluous
moment in a screenplay because the

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:30.300
attention of an audience is so short you
need to constantly be showing them what

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.450
the next step is what the next question
is what the next mystery is there's not

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:41.190
a lot of time for you to go on tangents
that are not directly related to the

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.899
story of the film now I talked about
before being able to go on tangents

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:52.199
through the eight sequence method in
that case even the tangents are directly

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:57.000
related to the main storyline in some
way so like in the case of misery when

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.059
we follow the sheriff as he tries to
figure out what's going on with this

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:04.409
missing guy and he ends up going to the
house we're not following the main

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:08.040
character but we're following a
character that's going to come in

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:14.040
contact with that character so it's it's
vital that every single scene be

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:20.100
directly related to the narrative spine
of it you know finding a way to make all

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.439
your characters unique is something
that's always good one of the common

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:30.299
tricks that I actually use and that's
actually very effective is if you write

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:35.310
a scene and if you cover the names of
all the characters if you read the scene

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.299
just the dialogue and you don't
immediately know who the characters are

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:43.259
based on what they're saying you need to
go back and figure out a way to write it

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:47.880
so that it's specific to that character
and that's a very effective way to kind

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.979
of improve the uniqueness of the
characters in your story you know

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:56.850
whether it's by changing the cadence of
how they talk whether it's changing the

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.079
point of view of what they're saying you
know figuring out why they're saying it

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:06.390
it helps to deepen who those characters
are those are those are some ways I

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:11.489
think that can help talk about sort of
you know like cutting the fat to speed

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:15.760
it up but one thing I love
and I feel like we've gotten too fast

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.480
paced with some of the movies and maybe
that's just because I'm in a different

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.879
generation you know but it seems like
it's a real art form to actually have it

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:30.069
be fast enough for television let's say
like sharp objects it felt like a slower

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:32.680
paced thing but I get it if you broke
down

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:37.959
just all the different scenes and and
sequences and and you know shots and

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:42.640
stuff it was probably very quick but
there was a slow feel to it and the last

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.959
black man in San Francisco it was a
slower feel to it but I mean I'm sure if

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:50.530
he boiled it down it was actually quite
fast paced but I think that's a real art

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:56.650
oh absolutely and part of that ties into
the way that you film the story you know

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.299
you think of like big little lies
there's that to me didn't feel all that

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:04.359
fast paced because you have a lot of
scenes you know with like Nicole Kidman

00:03:04.359 --> 00:03:10.299
for example in therapy those scenes even
though they're vital to the story they

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:15.579
don't lend themselves to snap bang fast
action storytelling so the the type of

00:03:15.579 --> 00:03:20.889
story that you're telling can help to
affect the pacing of it even if it is

00:03:20.889 --> 00:03:24.910
properly structured in the script the
way that you film it can have a big

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.359
effect on that that's true because I'm
thinking of like the different women in

00:03:28.359 --> 00:03:32.889
there and they're sort of they're
emotionals wine-drinking scene where the

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:36.129
beautiful ocean like a lot of those
season at least create scenes I'm like

00:03:36.129 --> 00:03:39.459
wow I wish I could live there but you
know so and then they're having this

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:44.069
sort of meltdown and a husband is like
honey it's okay you know and it was very

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:49.930
it felt very slow moving but I get it it
probably was was just a quick moment

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:54.609
yeah yeah and it's all the style the
filmmaker - you know I won one film I

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:59.530
think of a lot in terms of that is the
Big Lebowski so that film if you read

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:04.780
the script the script moves pretty quick
but if you watch the film the film takes

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:09.879
place in a lot of single shots and it
moves very the camera moves very slowly

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:14.290
as the characters progress through the
scene so that has a way of slowing

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:20.470
things down a little bit so even though
the script is very tight every scene

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:25.090
ties in directly to to what the main
story is even though like

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.230
can seem meandering because of who the
character is everything's there

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:34.030
but the way that the coen shoot it's
almost kind of theatrical where

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.389
everything is taking place within a
single frame and characters are moving

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:42.220
within and out without any cuts that can
give the feeling of a slower pace so

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:47.610
it's it's a filmmaking thing more than
it is a screenwriting thing okay

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.160
you

